d #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-118779"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-96711/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "f9efb605-ea2e-5276-951e-45dfc94d677e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96711/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "f6b8be8e-1100-512a-8661-62dfd15d9af2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96711/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "ced67b4c-1e73-5217-a34d-7acc4b3044ed" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96711/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "b9037e85-690a-57c7-810c-89240a8faf6c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96711/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "9ddfbb2f-742c-578c-9479-00d783882ec9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96711/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8661cba3-7048-5b7f-9533-d880c35c415e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-96711/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-96711/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-96711/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-96711/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-96711/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-96711/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-96711" :question/id #uuid "b527fd3f-b1f2-54f1-b985-21a0b1fa81ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-96711" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}=1$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-96711/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-96711"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-118869/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "d5066b58-78d8-5140-a9a4-8ca339b47771" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118869/selection/b1a9f2bb9f9bd59b66c906c9ab98446a26ec623744a7f92356bb121785f6cea5" :answer/id #uuid "4b58bfba-b2d3-55d1-9844-ac232b42d948" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118869/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "7f6e971a-90bd-5d56-bdbc-dbb5935fdfc4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118869/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "3401dc1c-be3b-5450-86fa-b713939750f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118869/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "a77cbbdd-237a-5e08-8415-610597bce1b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118869/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "88e20f1e-782b-5788-bac7-b40b22e346c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-118869/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-118869/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-118869/selection/b1a9f2bb9f9bd59b66c906c9ab98446a26ec623744a7f92356bb121785f6cea5" "q-118869/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-118869/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-118869/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-118869" :question/id #uuid "3c091681-9ae6-5d3e-9149-c42e1bb6941d" :question/math-academy-id "q-118869" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+{y}^{2}={x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-118869/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-118869"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-96713/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "8aa2c58b-83b1-5a1f-82ce-1b76a28a1da9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96713/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "2e8ac46e-b347-5f0e-909a-dcff80c5eab3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96713/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "6eeba983-720f-56dc-8b71-b7e0f41f1ff3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96713/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "d8fec451-4fb0-5dbe-ab3c-b5a486cef109" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96713/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "c5ec787f-53e6-5770-9220-2bf0503243ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96713/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "73c44b9f-1895-57d1-b493-bf687d31b16e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-96713/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-96713/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-96713/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-96713/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-96713/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-96713/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-96713" :question/id #uuid "21877f7e-3309-5578-9c06-511e21f96e25" :question/math-academy-id "q-96713" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${e}^{-y}{y}^{′}=-\\sqrt{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}-{y}^{3}=2{e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}=3\\sqrt{y}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-y}{y}^{′} & =-\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\sqrt{y}{e}^{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-\\sqrt{y}{e}^{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{y}^{3} & =2{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={y}^{3}+2{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={y}^{3}+2{e}^{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}} & =3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-96713/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-96713"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-118870/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "cfd9cbc5-5cc6-555c-8b8d-e057aa50623d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118870/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "e4a44460-7381-5aef-af91-96d09f592d66" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118870/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "1db39c90-0021-5858-9a36-892f0cfc695f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118870/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "3df41c3d-5ac4-52f6-a7bf-f5ab1a58da23" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118870/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "aa8d5d2a-7cdc-5f1c-aea0-e15081dbc3ce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118870/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d1ee4e6b-9bed-5358-88b0-838325134f05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-118870/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-118870/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-118870/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-118870/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-118870/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-118870/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-118870" :question/id #uuid "4a6041e3-4776-511a-b5c9-9dd819e86bef" :question/math-academy-id "q-118870" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={e}^{3-y}-2$\n- $y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=1$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+y)$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{3-y}-2.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-118870/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-118870"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-96715/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "54334491-13f1-5986-89d2-27af10a558a1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96715/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "0c98a91c-3913-54f1-b523-a0110cd22753" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96715/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "684496cd-8bc5-55f5-ab37-59d661096878" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96715/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "fbadf60a-49c0-515c-be47-e5818be7829b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96715/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "3f7628d8-e294-5ccb-b089-d4ffe9f9b156" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-96715/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bf9570b8-6ef7-57bd-9b36-2588c38d2c8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-96715/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-96715/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-96715/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-96715/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-96715/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-96715/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-96715" :question/id #uuid "b3ca412b-50e9-5db7-a51c-5819f837524c" :question/math-academy-id "q-96715" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\sqrt{2y}$\n- ${y}^{′}-{y}^{3}=3\\sqrt{y}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\sqrt{2y}.$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{y}^{3} & =3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={y}^{3}+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={y}^{3}+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-96715/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-96715"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-96986"] :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′},\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$\n- $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=2$\n- $4{y}^{′}-\\frac{1}{3}xy=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}=3{y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{′}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-118791/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "70d61d2a-27c7-5a37-af06-55a203b16f25" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118791/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "e94997a8-0a2e-5b11-84a6-6d30752c74f1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118791/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "113ab132-b40f-5ab4-be6a-6c9ba0884b3e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118791/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "777be402-ac0f-53da-98ec-0b10ae621300" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118791/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "012f77a9-3aa3-5515-bd22-99961b8ad332" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118791/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "40012c1d-87cf-5109-9a23-6aca313e1115" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-118791/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-118791/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-118791/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-118791/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-118791/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-118791/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-118791" :question/id #uuid "90a18919-f815-5b77-9ac2-909415c66f7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-118791" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x},\\,y(1)=6$\n- $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(4)=-3$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-118791/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-118791"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-95985"] :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $2x{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=1$\n- ${y}^{′}-2xy={e}^{y}$\n- $2x{y}^{″}={y}^{′},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2x{y}^{′}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2x{y}^{″}={y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-118790/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "905c956b-71ca-53d4-96fe-817ce32a0fee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118790/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "66878d39-e481-5c06-bc31-bdbfe95200e9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118790/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "5d533b47-95eb-5b1a-92f1-d16bec403fda" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118790/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "7f87b4c2-55f6-5a21-b85e-5eacbf4399f1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118790/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "3add1308-38cf-5b33-b911-8b52a18a6451" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118790/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "866fad75-1b84-5f9d-b6e3-33fb6596644c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-118790/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-118790/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-118790/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-118790/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-118790/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-118790/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-118790" :question/id #uuid "4db518e4-03ec-5f0b-bee4-e745f4578bc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-118790" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-9x,\\,y(1)=-4$\n- ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y,\\,y(0)=-3$\n- ${y}^{″}+y=3$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}=-9x$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=-4.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=-3.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-118790/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-118790"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-118787/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "24974ecf-0ec7-5319-af8e-c5ecd324b478" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118787/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "84525e64-3770-5710-800d-17ca14cb1df0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118787/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "511a2a26-f405-5752-9647-cc01b576f3e1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118787/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "e51c41a2-5a38-50c1-b64e-c02234a5c23a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118787/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "08307e15-cafd-5fa9-b4ce-b10cd892d7a2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118787/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f458c836-e756-5985-b647-d7229c4f9fa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-118787/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-118787/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-118787/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-118787/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-118787/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-118787/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-118787" :question/id #uuid "9102c868-96ec-5673-8dcc-be6cc7ee008d" :question/math-academy-id "q-118787" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-x=-7y,\\,y(2)=3$\n- ${x}^{4}{y}^{‴}+3y={2}^{y},\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{″}(0)=0$\n- $x{y}^{″}-6y=14,\\,y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}-x=-7y$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${x}^{4}{y}^{‴}+3y={2}^{y}$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,$ and ${y}^{″}(0)=0.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $x{y}^{″}-6y=14$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-118787/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-118787"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8459"] :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $2x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}+y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}=3x^2?$" :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3},$ which has order $3.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8460"] :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n\n- $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, $y'=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)= y^3.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}+2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)= \\dfrac{y^3+2}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)= 1+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8461"] :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n\n- $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n\n- $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\quad y(x_0) = y_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^2-3y' = y^3$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System II is also a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=e.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y'$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=3,\\, y'(2) = 2.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
